# Shell

The interactive shell on this machine is fish; scripts are bash. They do not
share syntax, so always be explicit about which one a command is written for.

## Running commands

- Write ad-hoc commands in POSIX or bash syntax. Do not rely on fish-only
  constructs (`and`, `or`, `set -x`, `$status`, abbreviations) unless the
  command is explicitly meant for fish.
- The interactive fish config defines aliases, abbreviations, and wrapper
  functions, so a bare command name may not be the binary it looks like. When
  the exact program matters, call it as `command <tool>` or by absolute path.
- A `cd` does not carry over between separate commands. Use absolute paths, or
  chain the `cd` into the same command.

## Writing scripts

- Start every bash script with `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, and add
  `set -euo pipefail` unless the surrounding code already installs its own
  error handling.
- Quote every variable expansion, prefer `[[ ]]` over `[ ]`, and `$(...)`
  over backticks.
- Build any bash script beyond a few lines on
  [dybatpho](https://github.com/dynamotn/dybatpho). Use the repository's own
  copy when it vendors one (often a submodule under `scripts/lib/dybatpho`),
  otherwise the shared checkout in `~/Working/personal/tools/dybatpho`.
  Source its `init.sh` (with `--modules <name>...` for anything beyond the
  core) and use its helpers — `dybatpho::die`, `dybatpho::is`,
  `dybatpho::opts::*`, the logging and trap handlers — instead of
  hand-rolling argument parsing, error handling, logging, or cleanup. Read
  its `AGENT.md` before reaching for a function you have not used yet. A
  one-off snippet that needs none of that may stay plain bash.
- Lint every shell script you write or change with `dyshellint <path>...`
  and fix what it reports before calling the work done. It runs ShellCheck
  and shfmt on top of its own rules, so do not settle for running those two
  alone. Silence a finding only when it is a deliberate choice, with an inline
  `# dyshellint disable=<CODE> <reason>` on the line above it.
- Add a `# shellcheck source=` directive when sourcing a library by a
  computed path.
- Fish scripts live in `*.fish` files and use fish syntax throughout. Never
  mix bash syntax into them, or fish syntax into a bash script.
